1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,199
AIS feeds do not wait politely for your
thread pool. Hundreds of vessels report
positions; operators open live maps; each
connection holds a servlet thread while
blocking I/O waits on the next read.

2
00:00:12,319 --> 00:00:25,000
Under fan-in, the thread-per-request model
collapses — not because the CPU is busy
computing, but because every open socket
owns a worker sitting idle on the network.
Reactive programming flips the default.

3
00:00:25,119 --> 00:00:36,679
Instead of returning a finished value, you
return a publisher that will emit values
later. The thread that accepts the
connection does not sit blocked on JDBC or
a remote call; it schedules work and moves
on.

4
00:00:36,799 --> 00:00:50,519
Subscribers request data; operators
transform streams; schedulers decide which
threads run which stages. The goal is
higher concurrency with fewer threads for
I/O-bound workloads — not magic speed for
CPU-bound math.

5
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:54,039
Project Reactor is the library Spring
WebFlux builds on.

6
00:00:54,160 --> 00:01:06,159
The Reactive Streams specification sits
underneath: Publisher, Subscriber,
Subscription, and Processor, with
backpressure as a first-class idea —
subscribers request only what they can
handle.

7
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:19,240
Reactor’s Mono and Flux are publishers
with a rich operator vocabulary. You
compose pipelines declaratively; nothing
runs until something subscribes. Look at
this java for a moment.

8
00:01:19,359 --> 00:01:28,120
Read that as assembly, not as imperative
steps that already executed. zipWith and
timeout describe a graph. Subscription
triggers demand.

9
00:01:28,239 --> 00:01:36,039
That laziness is why returning a Mono from
a WebFlux controller works — the framework
subscribes and wires the response when
data arrives.

10
00:01:36,159 --> 00:01:49,479
Reactive is not “async annotations on
servlet code.” A blocking Thread.sleep or
JDBC call inside a reactive operator still
blocks a thread — often an event-loop
thread you cannot afford to stall under
AIS load.

11
00:01:49,599 --> 00:02:00,839
The discipline is end-to-end non-blocking
I/O (R2DBC, reactive HTTP clients) or
explicit offloading of blocking work onto
bounded elastic schedulers.

12
00:02:00,959 --> 00:02:08,039
Mixing one blocking repository into a
WebFlux app is a classic production
footgun. When do you reach for this model?

13
00:02:08,219 --> 00:02:17,180
Many concurrent slow I/O dependencies,
streaming AIS positions, or gateways that
multiplex downstream calls. When do you
stay on Spring MVC?

14
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:29,920
Familiar blocking drivers, team expertise,
and workloads that never needed tens of
thousands of concurrent connections. Boot
can run either stack; picking both without
clear boundaries usually creates
confusion.

15
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:43,200
A misconception is equating reactive with
faster CPU-bound algorithms. Another is
treating subscribe() inside business code
as normal — in WebFlux you typically
return the publisher and let the framework
subscribe.

16
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:51,120
A third is ignoring cancellation: when an
operator closes a map, subscriptions
cancel; your pipeline should not ignore
that.

17
00:02:51,240 --> 00:03:04,879
Today we named the shift: publishers
instead of eager values, subscription as
the start gun, backpressure as part of the
contract. The smallest publisher shape —
zero or one element — is where most vessel
lookups live.

18
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:06,439
That shape is Mono.
